---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const wells = assertion('eighty-seven-wells-have-a-record')
const oneYear = assertion('eight-hundred-of-them-are-one-year')
const oneWell = assertion('eighty-nine-wells-and-one-instrument')
const fell = assertion('eleven-and-a-half-feet')
const faster = assertion('three-times-faster-than-any-neighbour')
const township = assertion('forty-three-wells-in-one-township')

const acres = assertion('seven-thousand-acres-of-wetland')
const dug = assertion('two-fifths-of-it-is-dug-or-dammed')
const marsh = assertion('the-marsh-is-four-hundred-acres')
const five = assertion('five-files-and-a-factor-of-two-thousand')
const ten = assertion('two-hydrography-files-ten-times-apart')
const lakes = assertion('nine-polygons-are-the-citys-water')
const left = assertion('what-is-left-of-the-swamp')

const fellSpec = bar(
  fell.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  fell.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Feet below land surface', labelValues: true },
)
const fasterSpec = bar(
  faster.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  faster.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Feet per year the water falls', labelValues: true },
)
const dugSpec = bar(
  dug.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  dug.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Acres carrying the modifier', labelValues: true },
)
const marshSpec = bar(
  marsh.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  marsh.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Acres', labelValues: true },
)
---

<Article slug="eleven-feet-down">
  <p>
    Allen County lies on the southern margin of the Great Black Swamp. It was ditched and tiled in
    the second half of the nineteenth century and the water went somewhere. Two federal files say
    where — one for what is under the ground and one for what is left on top of it — and neither
    had been read here.
  </p>

  <h2>Ninety wells and one instrument</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={wells} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={oneYear} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={township} />
  <p>
    Sixty-eight wells read five times across five days in one October is not a monitoring network.
    It is the shape of somebody pumping a well and watching what the water does in the wells around
    it. The file records the tape they used and the agency that held it and no reason at all.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={oneWell} open />

  <h2>Eleven and a half feet</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={fell} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="fell"
    title="Depth to water at AL-5, Shawnee Township"
    caption="Mean of the first three calendar years of the record against the mean of the last three. Deeper is drier: the water is further below the surface."
    columns={['Period', 'Feet below surface']}
    rows={fell.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(2)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="fell" set:html={JSON.stringify(fellSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={faster} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="faster"
    title="How fast the water falls, by county"
    caption="The same agency, the same aquifer, the same four-or-five readings a year. Allen County against the three fastest-falling of the eight wells around it."
    columns={['Well', 'Feet a year']}
    rows={faster.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(3)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="faster" set:html={JSON.stringify(fasterSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    One well is not a water table, and a well sealed under rock measures pressure rather than a
    surface. What the eight neighbours buy is narrower than it looks and worth saying exactly:
    whatever is lowering the water here is not lowering it in the six counties around this one, so
    the movement is not the instrument. That is the strongest thing a control taken from geography
    can ever say, and it leaves the reason open. Nobody publishes how much water is pumped out of
    Allen County.
  </p>

  <h2>What is left on top</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={acres} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={dug} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="dug"
    title="Mapped wetland by what was done to it"
    caption="Cowardin modifiers on the county's 7,361 acres. The layer's plain-English rollup drops all three and calls the result a Pond or a Lake."
    columns={['Modifier', 'Acres']}
    rows={dug.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="dug" set:html={JSON.stringify(dugSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={lakes} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={marsh} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="marsh"
    title="Marsh now against swamp forest at the first surveys"
    caption="Emergent wetland on the federal map today, against the elm-ash swamp forest the earliest land surveys of this county recorded."
    columns={['Measure', 'Acres']}
    rows={marsh.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString()])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="marsh" set:html={JSON.stringify(marshSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={left} />

  <h2>Five files, one piece of ground</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={five} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={ten} />
  <p>
    None of the five is wrong and none is the others' answer. Hydric soil is ground that formed
    under standing water and stays hydric after it is drained. Swamp forest is what a surveyor
    walking a section line in the 1820s wrote down. Wetland on a habitat map and a water body on a
    flow network are two different objects, which is why two federal polygon maps of the same
    county differ by a factor of ten on the same word. The useful question is never how much
    wetland there is. It is which file, and what it was built to answer.
  </p>
</Article>
